WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.387
Least privilege reduces blast radius.

2
00:00:02.387 --> 00:00:11.291
The database account used by a running application should have only the permissions the application needs for its normal responsibilities.

3
00:00:11.291 --> 00:00:20.646
Broad permissions may be convenient during development, but they increase the consequences of defects, misconfiguration, and unexpected behavior.

4
00:00:20.646 --> 00:00:24.646
Different tasks may require different accounts or permissions.

5
00:00:24.646 --> 00:00:33.807
Runtime access, migrations, reporting, maintenance, administration, and emergency operations do not necessarily need the same database rights.

6
00:00:33.807 --> 00:00:39.872
Separating those responsibilities can make releases safer and make operational review clearer.

7
00:00:39.872 --> 00:00:46.324
Connection strings, database credentials, certificates, and service identities are sensitive assets.

8
00:00:46.324 --> 00:00:54.711
They should be protected through secret-management mechanisms, managed identities, deployment systems, or other approved controls.

9
00:00:54.711 --> 00:01:03.679
They should not be hardcoded into source code, committed to repositories, pasted into tickets, printed in logs, or captured in screenshots.

10
00:01:03.679 --> 00:01:05.679
Environment separation matters.

11
00:01:05.679 --> 00:01:14.067
Development, test, staging, and production should not casually share credentials, data access permissions, or diagnostic settings.

12
00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:18.648
A development shortcut should not become a production permission model.

13
00:01:18.648 --> 00:01:23.228
Review database configuration as part of release and change management.

14
00:01:23.228 --> 00:01:26.841
Database permissions should be visible enough to review.

15
00:01:26.841 --> 00:01:40.390
Teams should know which application identity can access which database objects, which migrations can change schema, which reports can read broad data, and which administrative workflows require elevated access.

16
00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:43.293
Hidden permissions are hard to manage safely.

17
00:01:43.293 --> 00:01:48.003
Least privilege does not replace parameterization, but it complements it.

18
00:01:48.003 --> 00:01:54.197
If a query path behaves unexpectedly, narrower permissions can reduce what that path can affect.

19
00:01:54.197 --> 00:01:57.810
Strong prevention and bounded impact work best together.
